From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Let the mode be chosen again, and give the card the room it has

Every link was held at 1280x720 at 30 Hz to find out whether the format was
what kept breaking a cast. It was not: what kept breaking it was a group
sharing the lower band with the station it was already on, and the picture
came right when the two were separated rather than when the format changed.

So the mode is chosen again from what a receiver offers and what is being
shared, as it was meant to be. On this receiver that comes to 1920x1080 at
30 Hz, which it announces and which is twice the picture.

Twice the picture is given twice the rate. Five megabits were what a crowded
lower band would carry; the upper one carries what is asked of it, with less
than a hundredth of what is sent needing a second attempt and no frame yet
dropped on the way to the encoder. The rate control is left where it was,
answering to two frames.

A rate held steady rather than left to rise and fall stays, and so does the
cadence: both were measured, and neither was the thing that was wrong.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
@@ -246,26 +246,6 @@
   g_autoptr(GList) all = wfd_video_codec_get_resolutions (codec);
   WfdResolution *best = NULL;
 
-  /* TESTING ONLY. Hold every link at 1280x720 at 30 Hz, which is the format
-   * the accounts of what survives a Wi-Fi Direct link agree on, so that what
-   * a steadier one is worth here can be measured before anything is built to
-   * choose between them. A receiver that announces 720p at 60 Hz announces it
-   * at 30 Hz as well, and a source is free to send the slower of the two.
-   * Remove this patch to go back to choosing a mode. */
-  for (GList *item = all; item; item = item->next)
-    {
-      WfdResolution *candidate = (WfdResolution *) item->data;
-
-      if (candidate->width == 1280 && candidate->height == 720 &&
-          candidate->refresh_rate == 30 && !candidate->interlaced)
-        {
-          g_message ("WfdClient: Testing: holding the link at 1280x720@30.");
-          return candidate;
-        }
-    }
-
-  g_message ("WfdClient: Testing: the receiver does not announce 1280x720@30; choosing as before.");
-
   preferred = wfd_video_codec_get_resolutions_cea (codec);
   best = wfd_client_pick_resolution (preferred, width, height);
 
diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
@@ -134,9 +134,12 @@
    * it kept and the buffer that let a hard frame through without bursting. */
   [ELEMENT_X264]      = { .bitrate_kbit = 5 * 1024, .vbv_frames = 2 },
 
-  /* Not yet found. Started where the software encoder ended so that the two
-   * can be compared, and meant to be moved from here on what is seen. */
-  [ELEMENT_NVH264]    = { .bitrate_kbit = 5 * 1024, .vbv_frames = 2 },
+  /* Found on a link with room to spare: a radio of its own in the upper band,
+   * eight tenths of a percent of what it sends asked for twice, and nothing
+   * dropped on the way to the encoder. Five megabits were what a crowded
+   * lower band would carry; ten are what this one does, and a picture of
+   * twice the size needs them. */
+  [ELEMENT_NVH264]    = { .bitrate_kbit = 10 * 1024, .vbv_frames = 2 },
 
   /* Never measured. Left to the receiver's own maximum, as before. */
   [ELEMENT_OPENH264]  = { .bitrate_kbit = 0, .vbv_frames = 0 },
